Records — one-line immutable data classes.

A huge amount of code exists only to carry data from one place to another — a pair of coordinates, a row from a database, a response body. Written as an ordinary class, that just-data carrier drowns in boilerplate: private final fields, a constructor, an accessor for each field, and correct equals, hash code, and to string. A record collapses all of it into one line.

Here's the whole class: public record point, with an x and a y. That header — the record components, x and y — is the entire specification. And from it, the compiler generates everything. A private final field for each component. A canonical constructor that assigns them. An accessor for each component, named after it — so x and y, called as x-parens and y-parens. And notice: it's x, not get-X. Records drop the old get prefix. It also generates equals and hash code based on all the components, giving you value equality that honours the contract from module two. And a readable to string, like Point, x equals one, y equals two.

So you make a new point of one and two, ask for its x and get one, compare it to another point of one and two and get true — value equality, for free — and its to string reads cleanly. Fifty lines of hand-written, bug-prone boilerplate become a single header you can read at a glance.

Records are immutable by design. Every component is final, and there are no setters. A record's state is fixed at construction. To change one, you create a new one — so to move a point, you build a fresh point with x plus one, and the original is untouched. And that immutability is the point, not a limitation. Immutable values are safe to share across threads, safe as map keys and set members, because their hash code never changes, and easy to reason about, because nobody can mutate one behind your back.

What is a record underneath? It's a normal class that extends Record. So it can implement interfaces, declare extra methods, hold static fields and static factories, and be generic — a pair of any two types, for instance. What it cannot do is extend another class, because its superclass is fixed as Record, and it can't add mutable instance fields beyond its components. It's deliberately narrow. A record is a transparent carrier for an immutable tuple of values, and those restrictions are exactly what keep that promise honest.

So when do you use one? Reach for a record whenever a type's identity is its data, and that data doesn't change: data-transfer objects and API payloads, coordinates and money and ranges, a compound map key, or the return value when a method needs to hand back two things at once. Records also pair beautifully with the sealed types and pattern matching still to come in this module — a sealed family of records is the idiomatic way to model "one of these shapes" in modern Java. And the next section shows how to add validation and behaviour, without giving up that one-line spirit.
